<body>
<p class="p1"><span class="s1"></span><br></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">title: "How to Negotiate Contractor Quotes Without Being 'That' Homeowner" description: "Learn professional negotiation tactics to lower contractor costs by 10-20% without damaging the relationship or compromising quality." date: 2024-02-03 author: Ungouge Team keywords: ["negotiate contractor quote", "lower contractor price", "contractor negotiation tactics", "how to negotiate with contractors", "reduce renovation costs", "contractor pricing negotiation"]</span></p>
<p class="p1"><span class="s1"></span><br></p>
<h1 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 16.1px 0.0px; font: 24.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #138756; -webkit-text-stroke: #138756"><span class="s1"><b>How to Negotiate Contractor Quotes Without Being "That" Homeowner</b></span></h1>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">You've got the quote. The number is higher than you hoped. Now what?</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Most homeowners either: 1. <b>Accept it without question</b> (and possibly overpay by thousands) 2. <b>Try to haggle aggressively</b> (and annoy the contractor into walking away) 3. <b>Just get more quotes</b> (hoping someone will be cheaper by chance)</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">There's a better way.</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Smart negotiation isn't about being cheap—it's about being informed.</b> When you understand where costs come from and which line items have flexibility, you can negotiate effectively while maintaining a positive working relationship.</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Here's how to do it right.</span></p>
<p class="p1"><span class="s1"></span><br></p>
<h2 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.9px 0.0px; font: 18.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0f6745; -webkit-text-stroke: #0f6745"><span class="s1"><b>Step 1: Understand What's Negotiable (and What Isn't)</b></span></h2>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Not all costs are created equal. Some are fixed. Some have wiggle room.</span></p>
<h3 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.0px 0.0px; font: 14.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0d4e36; -webkit-text-stroke: #0d4e36"><span class="s1"><b>✅ Usually Negotiable:</b></span></h3>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>1. Contingency Percentage</b> - Standard: 10-15% - Often inflated: 20-25% - <b>Negotiation angle:</b> "I see a 20% contingency. Industry standard is 10-15%. Can we bring this down?"</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>2. Material Markup</b> - Typical: 20-35% - Sometimes excessive: 50%+ - <b>Negotiation angle:</b> "I'm seeing this material listed at $X supplier cost with a 50% markup. Can we work with a more standard 25-30%?"</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>3. Profit Margin</b> - Reasonable: 10-20% on top of costs - High: 25-35% - <b>Negotiation angle:</b> Not directly negotiable, but understanding it helps you evaluate total quote fairness</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>4. Labor Rates (Sometimes)</b> - If quoted hourly, you can discuss rate - If quoted as lump sum, harder to negotiate - <b>Negotiation angle:</b> "I'm seeing $85/hour for general labor. Local prevailing rate is closer to $60-65. Can we adjust?"</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>5. Scope Adjustments</b> - Removing non-essential elements - Phasing work over time - <b>Negotiation angle:</b> "What if we skip the decorative trim package and do DIY paint prep—what would that save?"</span></p>
<h3 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.0px 0.0px; font: 14.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0d4e36; -webkit-text-stroke: #0d4e36"><span class="s1"><b>❌ Usually NOT Negotiable:</b></span></h3>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>1. Permits and Fees</b> - Set by local government - Contractor can't discount these - <b>Red flag:</b> If a contractor offers to "skip permits to save money," RUN.</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>2. Material Costs (for specialty items)</b> - Custom orders, specialty materials - Contractor has little control over supplier pricing - <b>Exception:</b> Bulk materials (lumber, drywall) sometimes have flexibility</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>3. Insurance and Licensing Costs</b> - These are real overhead - Asking a contractor to cut corners here hurts you both</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>4. Subcontractor Costs (mostly)</b> - If the GC is hiring licensed electricians/plumbers, those rates are market-driven - Some flexibility on scheduling (off-season = lower rates)</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>5. Disposal and Dumpster Fees</b> - These are pass-through costs - Actual receipts are verifiable</span></p>
<p class="p1"><span class="s1"></span><br></p>
<h2 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.9px 0.0px; font: 18.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0f6745; -webkit-text-stroke: #0f6745"><span class="s1"><b>Step 2: Do Your Homework BEFORE Negotiating</b></span></h2>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Walking into a negotiation blind is like playing poker without looking at your cards.</span></p>
<h3 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.0px 0.0px; font: 14.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0d4e36; -webkit-text-stroke: #0d4e36"><span class="s1"><b>What You Need to Know:</b></span></h3>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>1. Market Rates for Materials</b></span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Use resources like: - <b>Local supplier websites</b> (Lowe's, Home Depot for standard materials) - <b>Specialty supplier quotes</b> (ask for pricing on specific items) - <b>Online cost databases</b> (HomeWyse, RSMeans)</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Example:</b></span></p>
<blockquote style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 12.0px 0.0px; font: 12.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #535353; -webkit-text-stroke: #535353"><span class="s1">"I looked up the quartz countertop you specified (Cambria Brittanicca). Wholesale is $55/sq ft. You're quoting $95/sq ft installed. That's a $40 markup, which seems high for fabrication and install. Industry average is $60-70/sq ft installed. Can we find middle ground?"</span></blockquote>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>2. Labor Rates in Your Area</b></span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Check: - Bureau of Labor Statistics data for your region - Local contractor forums/groups - Conversations with other homeowners who've done similar projects</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Example:</b></span></p>
<blockquote style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 12.0px 0.0px; font: 12.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #535353; -webkit-text-stroke: #535353"><span class="s1">"I spoke with three neighbors who've done kitchen remodels recently. Labor rates ranged from $50-65/hour for skilled trades. Your quote seems to assume $80/hour. Can you break that down?"</span></blockquote>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>3. Project Scope Comparisons</b></span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Get at least two other quotes for comparison (even if you prefer one contractor).</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Why this works:</b> - Shows you're informed, not just price shopping - Gives you leverage ("Contractor B included X in base price, but you have it as an add-on") - Helps you spot outliers (if one quote is 40% higher, you need to know why)</span></p>
<p class="p1"><span class="s1"></span><br></p>
<h2 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.9px 0.0px; font: 18.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0f6745; -webkit-text-stroke: #0f6745"><span class="s1"><b>Step 3: Frame the Conversation Professionally</b></span></h2>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Bad approach:</b></span></p>
<blockquote style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 12.0px 0.0px; font: 12.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #535353; -webkit-text-stroke: #535353"><span class="s1">"Your quote is way too high. I need you to cut $5,000 or I'm going with someone else."</span></blockquote>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Good approach:</b></span></p>
<blockquote style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 12.0px 0.0px; font: 12.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #535353; -webkit-text-stroke: #535353"><span class="s1">"I appreciate the detailed quote. I've done some research on material costs and labor rates in the area, and I'd like to discuss a few line items to make sure I understand the pricing. Can we walk through this together?"</span></blockquote>
<h3 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.0px 0.0px; font: 14.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0d4e36; -webkit-text-stroke: #0d4e36"><span class="s1"><b>The Magic Phrase:</b></span></h3>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>"Help me understand..."</b></span></p>
<ul class="ul1">
  <li class="li7"><span class="s2"></span><span class="s1">"Help me understand why the contingency is at 20% instead of the standard 10-15%."</span></li>
  <li class="li7"><span class="s2"></span><span class="s1">"Help me understand the difference between your material quote and the supplier pricing I'm seeing."</span></li>
  <li class="li7"><span class="s2"></span><span class="s1">"Help me understand how you arrived at this labor estimate."</span></li>
</ul>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">This does three things: 1. <b>Assumes good intent</b> (maybe there's a legitimate reason for higher costs) 2. <b>Positions you as informed</b> (you've done research) 3. <b>Opens dialogue</b> instead of creating confrontation</span></p>
<p class="p1"><span class="s1"></span><br></p>
<h2 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.9px 0.0px; font: 18.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0f6745; -webkit-text-stroke: #0f6745"><span class="s1"><b>Step 4: Negotiate Strategically (Not Desperately)</b></span></h2>
<h3 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.0px 0.0px; font: 14.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0d4e36; -webkit-text-stroke: #0d4e36"><span class="s1"><b>Tactic #1: The "Scope Adjustment" Approach</b></span></h3>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Instead of asking for a discount, ask what you can remove or change to hit your budget.</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Example:</b></span></p>
<blockquote style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 12.0px 0.0px; font: 12.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #535353; -webkit-text-stroke: #535353"><span class="s1">"My target budget is $30,000, and your quote came in at $35,000. If we switched from quartz to granite countertops and I handled the painting myself, could we get closer to my number?"</span></blockquote>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Why this works:</b> - Shows you're flexible, not just cheap - Gives the contractor control over how to reduce costs - Often reveals which line items have the most markup</span></p>
<h3 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.0px 0.0px; font: 14.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0d4e36; -webkit-text-stroke: #0d4e36"><span class="s1"><b>Tactic #2: The "Matching" Approach</b></span></h3>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">If you have multiple quotes, use them strategically (without playing contractors off each other aggressively).</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Example:</b></span></p>
<blockquote style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 12.0px 0.0px; font: 12.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #535353; -webkit-text-stroke: #535353"><span class="s1">"I have another quote that's $4,000 lower, but I prefer working with you based on your reputation and communication. Is there any flexibility in your pricing to get closer to that range?"</span></blockquote>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Why this works:</b> - Flatters the contractor (you <i>want</i> them, not just their price) - Gives them a target to match - Leaves room for compromise (they might meet you halfway)</span></p>
<h3 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.0px 0.0px; font: 14.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0d4e36; -webkit-text-stroke: #0d4e36"><span class="s1"><b>Tactic #3: The "Value-Add" Approach</b></span></h3>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Ask for extras instead of discounts.</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Example:</b></span></p>
<blockquote style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 12.0px 0.0px; font: 12.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #535353; -webkit-text-stroke: #535353"><span class="s1">"If the $28,000 price is firm, would you be willing to include the backsplash installation (currently listed as $1,200 add-on) at no extra charge?"</span></blockquote>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Why this works:</b> - Contractors often have more flexibility on labor (time) than price (cash) - Padding is often in "extras"—asking for them to be included exposes inflated optional pricing</span></p>
<h3 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.0px 0.0px; font: 14.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0d4e36; -webkit-text-stroke: #0d4e36"><span class="s1"><b>Tactic #4: The "Payment Terms" Approach</b></span></h3>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Offer favorable payment terms in exchange for a discount.</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Example:</b></span></p>
<blockquote style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 12.0px 0.0px; font: 12.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #535353; -webkit-text-stroke: #535353"><span class="s1">"If I pay 50% upfront instead of the standard 30%, could you reduce the total by 5%?"</span></blockquote>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Why this works:</b> - Cash flow is valuable to contractors - Faster payment = less financing cost for them - <b>WARNING:</b> Only do this with a fully licensed, insured, and reputable contractor. Never pay 100% upfront.</span></p>
<h3 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.0px 0.0px; font: 14.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0d4e36; -webkit-text-stroke: #0d4e36"><span class="s1"><b>Tactic #5: The "Referral Incentive" Approach</b></span></h3>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Offer to refer future customers in exchange for a discount.</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Example:</b></span></p>
<blockquote style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 12.0px 0.0px; font: 12.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #535353; -webkit-text-stroke: #535353"><span class="s1">"I'm active in our neighborhood Facebook group and NextDoor. If you give me a fair price and do great work, I'll actively refer you to others. Would you consider a 10% 'referral partner' discount?"</span></blockquote>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Why this works:</b> - Contractors value word-of-mouth marketing - It's a win-win (you save money, they get future business) - Shows you're thinking long-term, not transactional</span></p>
<p class="p1"><span class="s1"></span><br></p>
<h2 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.9px 0.0px; font: 18.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0f6745; -webkit-text-stroke: #0f6745"><span class="s1"><b>Step 5: Know When to Walk Away</b></span></h2>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Not every quote is negotiable. Sometimes a contractor is:</span></p>
<ul class="ul1">
  <li class="li7"><span class="s2"><b></b></span><span class="s1"><b>Already priced fairly</b> (and pushing back will damage the relationship)</span></li>
  <li class="li7"><span class="s2"><b></b></span><span class="s1"><b>Too expensive for your budget</b> (and no amount of negotiation will bridge the gap)</span></li>
  <li class="li7"><span class="s2"><b></b></span><span class="s1"><b>Padding excessively</b> (and negotiating won't fix a trust issue)</span></li>
</ul>
<h3 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.0px 0.0px; font: 14.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0d4e36; -webkit-text-stroke: #0d4e36"><span class="s1"><b>Red Flags That Mean "Don't Hire This Contractor":</b></span></h3>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">❌ <b>Refuses to itemize costs</b></span></p>
<blockquote style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 12.0px 0.0px; font: 12.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #535353; -webkit-text-stroke: #535353"><span class="s1">"I don't break down labor and materials. This is my price, take it or leave it."</span></blockquote>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">❌ <b>Gets defensive when asked about pricing</b></span></p>
<blockquote style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 12.0px 0.0px; font: 12.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #535353; -webkit-text-stroke: #535353"><span class="s1">"If you don't trust me, hire someone else."</span></blockquote>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">❌ <b>Can't explain high costs</b></span></p>
<blockquote style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 12.0px 0.0px; font: 12.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #535353; -webkit-text-stroke: #535353"><span class="s1">"That's just what it costs. I don't set the prices."</span></blockquote>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">❌ <b>Pressures you to sign immediately</b></span></p>
<blockquote style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 12.0px 0.0px; font: 12.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #535353; -webkit-text-stroke: #535353"><span class="s1">"This price is only good if you sign today."</span></blockquote>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">❌ <b>Asks for large upfront payments</b> (50%+ before work starts)</span></p>
<blockquote style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 12.0px 0.0px; font: 12.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #535353; -webkit-text-stroke: #535353"><span class="s1">"I need $20,000 down to order materials." <i>(Real material orders rarely exceed $5-8K for most projects)</i></span></blockquote>
<h3 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.0px 0.0px; font: 14.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0d4e36; -webkit-text-stroke: #0d4e36"><span class="s1"><b>Green Flags That Mean "This Contractor Is Professional":</b></span></h3>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">✅ <b>Willing to explain costs</b></span></p>
<blockquote style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 12.0px 0.0px; font: 12.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #535353; -webkit-text-stroke: #535353"><span class="s1">"Sure, let me break down the quartz pricing. Here's the supplier cost, here's fabrication, here's installation labor."</span></blockquote>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">✅ <b>Open to adjustments</b></span></p>
<blockquote style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 12.0px 0.0px; font: 12.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #535353; -webkit-text-stroke: #535353"><span class="s1">"If budget is tight, we could switch to a less expensive tile and save $2,000."</span></blockquote>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">✅ <b>Transparent about markup</b></span></p>
<blockquote style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 12.0px 0.0px; font: 12.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #535353; -webkit-text-stroke: #535353"><span class="s1">"I mark up materials 25% to cover ordering, handling, and warranty support."</span></blockquote>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">✅ <b>Provides references and proof of past work</b></span></p>
<blockquote style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 12.0px 0.0px; font: 12.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #535353; -webkit-text-stroke: #535353"><span class="s1">"Here are three recent kitchen projects I've done. Feel free to call the homeowners."</span></blockquote>
<p class="p1"><span class="s1"></span><br></p>
<h2 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.9px 0.0px; font: 18.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0f6745; -webkit-text-stroke: #0f6745"><span class="s1"><b>Step 6: Get Everything in Writing</b></span></h2>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Once you've negotiated, document the agreed-upon price and scope in a written contract.</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Must-haves in the contract:</b> - Total cost (broken down by labor, materials, permits, etc.) - Payment schedule (never more than 50% upfront) - Timeline with milestones - Scope of work (detailed list of what's included) - Change order process (how additional costs are handled) - Warranty terms - Cancellation/termination clause</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Pro tip:</b> If a contractor won't provide a written contract, don't hire them. Period.</span></p>
<p class="p1"><span class="s1"></span><br></p>
<h2 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.9px 0.0px; font: 18.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0f6745; -webkit-text-stroke: #0f6745"><span class="s1"><b>Example Negotiation Script (Putting It All Together)</b></span></h2>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Setup:</b> You received a $32,000 quote for a bathroom remodel. Your budget is $28,000.</span></p>
<p class="p1"><span class="s1"></span><br></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>You:</b><br>
"Hi [Contractor Name], thanks for the detailed quote. I've reviewed it carefully and done some research on pricing. I'd like to discuss a few line items to make sure I understand the costs before moving forward."</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Contractor:</b><br>
"Sure, what questions do you have?"</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>You:</b><br>
"First, I see the contingency is listed at 20%. I've read that 10-15% is more standard for bathroom projects. Can you help me understand why it's higher in this case?"</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Contractor:</b><br>
"That's fair. We usually do 15%, but this is an older home and we're worried about hidden issues behind the walls."</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>You:</b><br>
"That makes sense. What if we reduce it to 15% and agree that any unexpected costs will go through a formal change order process before proceeding? That way we're both protected."</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Contractor:</b><br>
"I can do that. That saves you about $1,600."</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>You:</b><br>
"Great. Second question: the tile is quoted at $12 per square foot installed. I found the same tile at [Supplier] for $4.50 per square foot. That's a $7.50 markup per square foot, which seems high. Can we break that down?"</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Contractor:</b><br>
"That $12 includes material cost, labor to install, grout, thin-set, and waterproofing membrane. The tile itself is $4.50, but installation materials and labor add up."</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>You:</b><br>
"Got it. So the tile material is $4.50, and installation is $7.50/sq ft in labor and materials?"</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Contractor:</b><br>
"Correct."</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>You:</b><br>
"That's helpful to understand. Is there any flexibility if I source the tile myself to save on markup?"</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Contractor:</b><br>
"I'd prefer to source it because then I'm responsible for warranty and matching. But I could reduce my markup from 30% to 20% if that helps."</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>You:</b><br>
"That would save about $400. I appreciate that."</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>You:</b><br>
"Last item: my budget is $28,000. With the contingency reduction and tile markup adjustment, we're at $30,400. If I pay 50% upfront instead of the standard 30%, could you bring the total to $28,500?"</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Contractor:</b><br>
"Let me think... I can do $29,000 final price with 50% upfront. That's the best I can do."</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>You:</b><br>
"I appreciate you working with me. Let me review the updated quote and I'll get back to you tomorrow."</span></p>
<p class="p1"><span class="s1"></span><br></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Result:</b><br>
- Original quote: $32,000 - Negotiated quote: $29,000 - <b>Savings: $3,000 (9.4% reduction)</b> - Relationship: intact and professional</span></p>
<p class="p1"><span class="s1"></span><br></p>
<h2 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.9px 0.0px; font: 18.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0f6745; -webkit-text-stroke: #0f6745"><span class="s1"><b>The Bottom Line</b></span></h2>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Negotiating contractor quotes isn't about being difficult—it's about being <b>informed and assertive</b>.</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Do:</b> - Research market rates before negotiating - Ask questions to understand costs - Propose scope adjustments to fit your budget - Frame requests professionally - Get everything in writing</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Don't:</b> - Haggle aggressively without data - Ask contractors to cut corners on quality or safety - Make ultimatums unless you're willing to walk away - Accept vague pricing or refusals to itemize - Pay more than 50% upfront</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>The goal:</b> Pay a fair price for quality work while building a good relationship with your contractor.</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Use data, ask smart questions, and negotiate with confidence.</span></p>
<p class="p1"><span class="s1"></span><br></p>
